#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "Core/FPMFixContract.h"

/**
 * SCHEMATIC NULL GUARD — refuses a schematic query that vanilla is about to die on, and SAYS SO EVERY
 * TIME. Design P3.10(a).
 *
 * Ant, 2026-08-08 (R59), with the crash numbers in front of her: *"Add a narrow null-guard, See if we
 * can fix the issue at the source aswell"* — she selected BOTH options, superseding the earlier
 * diagnostics-only ruling. This file is (a). The source repair is (b) and is NOT code: it is the
 * upstream report plus the removal test, and it is what eventually makes this file deletable.
 *
 * ★ WHY THERE IS A GUARD HERE AT ALL WHEN THE PROBE'S HEADER ARGUES AGAINST ONE.
 * That argument was made against a guard that shipped SILENTLY and was narrowed three times by authors
 * who could not see it working. It is not an argument against guarding; it is an argument against
 * guarding blind. Everything that made the old one unfalsifiable is inverted here: it logs the first
 * sighting of every distinct class unthrottled, it counts refusals AND pass-throughs so a dead guard is
 * visibly dead (design §3.3), and the probe stays armed BESIDE it still naming origins. If this guard
 * is wrong, the log says which schematic it was wrong about, by name, on the first frame it happens.
 *
 * ★ THE EVIDENCE, 6/6 FROM BYTES (critic R2-P4, re-verified R3).
 * All six schematic dumps in the 31-dump corpus crash at the identical site
 * `UFGSchematic::CanGiveAccessToSchematic() FGSchematic.cpp:408`, all `EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION`
 * reading `0x00000000000002c0`. Three full stacks read (37681B11, A981D1D4, E6D4E9FE) and three top
 * frames (4DB929D6, 5DC04A67, 9DF7FBEE). Callers converge through
 * `AFGSchematicManager::Internal_CommitCurrentSchematicTransaction` (:801) — the GRANT path, not a
 * query. A `SUBSCRIBE_METHOD` hook runs BEFORE the body (hooking.adoc:129-130), so an entry check on
 * the argument intercepts every one of those paths: 6/6 coverage, not a sample.
 *
 * ★ AND THE MECHANISM IS NOW THE FUNCTION'S OWN DOCUMENTED CONTRACT, not our inference.
 * `FGSchematic.h:158` — *"Checks for events and if mDependenciesBlocksSchematicAccess is true we check
 * that all dependencies are met as well"*. `mDependenciesBlocksSchematicAccess` is an instance member,
 * `FGSchematic.h:267`. The function is STATIC and takes a `TSubclassOf`, so the ONLY way it reads that
 * member is off the class default object. A null CDO puts that read at the member's offset from a null
 * base — which is the shape of `0x2c0` exactly. Corroborating, from the log census: four sibling
 * statics in the same family (`GetUnlocks` 19, `GetIsPlayerSpecific` 16, `IsRepeatPurchasesAllowed` 16,
 * `GetCost` 9 — 60/session) emit CSS's own `nullpeter` warning on this same input and survive. They
 * have the null check. This one does not, and dies. That is a missing guard at the source, which is
 * precisely what (b) reports upstream.
 *
 * ⚠ THIS DOES NOT CLAIM THE CRASH IS FIXED, AND THE ORIGIN STATUS SAYS SO IN THE BOOT LOG.
 * Per Ant's standing Q1 rule, "fixed" belongs only beside `OriginNamed`. The origin here is NAMED — a
 * broken ContentLib milestone, `Schematic_CustomizerTest_C`, mounted from `/SS_Mod/` — but not
 * CONFIRMED as the whole story: one dump (A981D1D4) carries no mod frame in its callstack at all. The
 * honest reading is that broken authored data reaches a vanilla function that forgot to check, and we
 * are holding the door shut while (b) goes after the data.
 *
 * ★ WHAT IT DOES, EXACTLY
 *  - null class            -> refuse, return false, count, name it (first fire unthrottled)
 *  - class with null CDO   -> refuse, return false, count, name the class (first fire unthrottled)
 *  - anything else         -> NOTHING. No Override, no Cancel. Vanilla answers, unmodified.
 *
 * `Override(false)` rather than `Cancel()`, because `Cancel` is only defined for the void specialisation
 * (`NativeHookManager.h:165`); a `Result`-returning scope stops the chain by supplying the answer
 * (`:211`). `false` is the conservative answer and the one consistent with the siblings: a schematic
 * whose data cannot be read cannot have its access requirements evaluated, so the safe report is "no",
 * exactly as `mDependenciesBlocksSchematicAccess` being unreadable would imply. Answering `true` would
 * be the old mod's forced-TRUE override, which is dead and stays dead.
 *
 * ⚠ ARM ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: THE PROBE MUST ARM FIRST.
 * `TCallScope::Override` sets `bForwardCall = false`, and the chain driver only advances to the next
 * handler `if (HandlerPtr == CachePtr && bForwardCall)` (`NativeHookManager.h:211-226`). So the first
 * handler that overrides silences every FPM handler registered behind it. Arming this guard ahead of
 * the probe would blind the probe on exactly the calls it exists to name — the opposite of R59, which
 * says the probe stays and keeps naming origins. StartupModule arms probe-then-guard and says why.
 *
 * ⚠ AND IT MUST NOT COST FRAMES. Same hot path as the probe, same reason: the HUB and the build-menu
 * search walk schematics, and version 0.58.54 of the old mod froze the game logging on every call. The
 * pass-through path here is one atomic increment and one pointer compare. Nothing else — no string, no
 * lock, no allocation — is reached unless the argument is actually broken.
 */
class FFPMSchematicNullGuard final : public IFPMFix
{
public:
	static FFPMSchematicNullGuard& Get();

	virtual const TCHAR* Name() const override { return TEXT("schematic-guard"); }

	/*
	 * Schematic grants are SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE — the observed crashes are on the grant path through
	 * `AFGSchematicManager::Internal_CommitCurrentSchematicTransaction`, which is where the server decides
	 * a milestone. A guard gated off the dedicated server would do nothing on the machine that runs the
	 * code it guards. `Any`, and not by default: by that argument.
	 */
	virtual EFPMFixSide Side() const override { return EFPMFixSide::Any; }

	/** Guard: the broken schematic data is another author's, and the missing null check is CSS's. Neither is
	 *  ours to own — we prevent the harm and (b) chases both. */
	virtual EFPMOriginStatus OriginStatus() const override { return EFPMOriginStatus::Guard; }

	/** Its OWN channel, not the probe's. Silencing the probe's per-call chatter must never silence a
	 *  refusal — a guard that went quiet while still changing answers is the shape R59 explicitly refused
	 *  ("it is a guard, not a suppression"). */
	virtual FPMDiag::EChannel Channel() const override { return FPMDiag::EChannel::SchematicGuard; }

	virtual void Arm() override;

	/** True once the hook is installed. The self-test refuses to run without it — see the .cpp. */
	static bool IsArmed();

	/** Refusals so far, and calls passed through untouched. The §3.3 denominator pair: a guard whose
	 *  refusal count is 0 against a large pass-through count is visibly doing nothing, which is the state
	 *  we want (b) to produce and want to be able to SEE it produce. */
	static void GetCounts(int32& OutRefusedNullClass, int32& OutRefusedNullCdo, int32& OutPassedThrough);
};
